Classification
This content is only supported in a Feishu Docs

---
1. unsafe impl Send: FP
unsafe impl<A: Array> Send for StackVec<A>
where
    A::Item: Send
{}
Rudra complains because the type physically contains:
pub data: MaybeUninit<A>
but the impl only requires A::Item: Send, not A: Send.
For this abstraction, A is intended to be only backing storage for A::Item; StackVec manually manages the initialized element prefix and suppresses ordinary destruction of the backing array. The crate-provided implementations are exclusively ordinary arrays:
unsafe impl<T> Array for [T; N] {
    type Item = T;
    ...
}
For [T; N], the array is Send exactly when T is Send, so A::Item: Send is sufficient for every provided implementation.
This is also the design still used by smallvec, from which this code was derived: its public SmallVec<A> similarly implements Send based on A::Item: Send. (Docs.rs)
Caveat
The Array safety contract is poorly documented. A downstream user could write a strange unsafe impl Array for a type containing unrelated non-Send state. But constructing such an implementation already requires unsafe code and would violate the intended “fixed-size array backing storage” abstraction.
Therefore, for normal safe-client soundness analysis:
Send report: FP
Reason: Rudra does not understand the Array → Array::Item relationship.
A conservative API-hardening change would nevertheless be:
unsafe impl<A> Send for StackVec<A>
where
    A: Array + Send,
    A::Item: Send,
{}
That would eliminate ambiguity around custom Array implementations.

---
2. insert_many: Rudra panic-safety report is FP
The dangerous-looking sequence is:
ptr::copy(...);       // duplicates trailing non-Copy elements
self.set_len(0);

let mut guard = DropOnPanic { ... };

let element = iter.next(); // arbitrary user code, can panic
ptr::write(...);

Older smallvec::insert_many implementations really did have double-free bugs when Iterator::next() panicked after elements had been copied. (RustSec)
But this version contains a deliberate cleanup guard:
struct DropOnPanic<T> {
    start: *mut T,
    skip: Range<usize>,
    len: usize,
}

impl<T> Drop for DropOnPanic<T> {
    fn drop(&mut self) {
        for i in 0..self.len {
            if !self.skip.contains(&i) {
                ptr::drop_in_place(self.start.add(i));
            }
        }
    }
}

Its invariant is correct:
1. The original tail is copied rightward.
2. self.len is set to zero so StackVec::drop cannot double-drop anything.
3. skip initially covers the duplicated/uninitialized insertion gap.
4. After each successful ptr::write, skip.start advances, making that newly initialized element eligible for cleanup.
5. If the iterator returns too few elements, the shifted tail is copied backward.
6. Only after duplicates and gaps are removed is the real length restored and the guard forgotten.
If iter.next() panics, the guard drops exactly:
- the prefix before index;
- successfully inserted elements;
- the shifted copy of the original tail;
while skipping the stale duplicate slots.
Therefore Rudra’s inferred pattern—
ptr::copy duplicates ownership
→ arbitrary iterator method panics
→ double drop
—is blocked by DropOnPanic.
Incorrect size_hint
This implementation also handles both directions of an incorrect hint:
- fewer items than the lower bound: move the tail backward;
- more items than the lower bound: insert remaining elements one by one with capacity assertions.
The crate’s older extend implementation had a real size_hint vulnerability, but that issue was patched in versions >=1.0.9; version 1.1.1 contains the non-optimized, checked extend implementation. (RustSec)
Recommended classification:
insert_many Rudra report: FP
Reason: panic guard correctly handles copied ownership.
Location-level caveat
Because StackVec::length is public, safe code can corrupt it. In an optimized build, a maliciously set length may make arithmetic wrap and allow insert_many or other methods to reach unsafe pointer operations with invalid counts. That is a different root cause—the broken public invariant—not the iterator-panic flow Rudra reports. The official advisory specifically identifies the public length field and unsafe methods trusting it. (OSV)
For a benchmark that labels only whether the function location can reach UB:
insert_many: TP-location / root-cause mismatch
For evaluating Rudra’s actual reported hypothesis:
insert_many: FP
I recommend the latter for Rudra precision measurements.

